title: Bloody March in Berlin
subtitle: The Berlin councils call a general strike.
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1919 and month = 3 and crisis = 0
max-visits: 1

= Bloody March in Berlin

On 3 March the general assembly of the Berlin workers' councils called a general strike for the recognition of the councils and the release of political prisoners. The Social Democrats voted for it, then left the strike committee because our delegates refused to sit with them. The city stopped. In the east, around the Alexanderplatz, crowds plundered shops, and soldiers of the People's Naval Division began handing out rifles.

Noske's troops are already moving in. [? if luxemburg_alive = 1 : Luxemburg, still in hiding, writes that the strike must not become a second January. ?][? if luxemburg_alive = 0 : Jogiches, who now holds the Zentrale together, writes that the strike must not become a second January. ?] The young men in Lichtenberg and Friedrichshain are not listening.

- @unarmed: Back the strike, but no armed fighting.
- @rising: Arm the strike and fight.
- @with_spd: Let the Social Democrats back into the strike committee.

@unarmed
subtitle: The Spartacist line. Some will fight anyway.
on-arrival: march_line = 1; worker_support += 3; repression += 6; luxemburgist_satisfaction += 5; offensive_satisfaction -= 3; lean_luxemburgist += 2; district_berlin_members -= 0.3

On 5 March *Die Rote Fahne* tells the workers to stay out of the fighting. The paper is banned the next day.

- @terror: Continue...

@rising
subtitle: [? if lock_uprising = 1 : Against the party line: costs Unity. ?] The Left's and the Left Radicals' answer. Noske has artillery, aircraft and orders to shoot.
choose-if: lock_uprising < 2
unavailable-subtitle: The [+ leading_faction_adj +] line forbids an armed rising.
